            <ArticleTitle>Coronary-coronary bypass using vein graft on a beating heart in a patient with porcelain aorta.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>54-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>There is increased risk of systemic embolism during cardiopulmonary bypass in patients with a severely atherosclerotic ascending aorta. We report a coronary-coronary bypass in a 74-year-old man with a porcelain aorta. He underwent a proximal right coronary-distal right coronary artery bypass with a saphenous vein graft, combined with a pedicled arterial graft (left internal mammary artery) to the left anterior descending artery, in the presence of a beating heart without cardiopulmonary bypass. The patient survived without evidence of perioperative myocardial infarction or cerebrovascular accident. One year later, follow-up angiography showed graft patency with good distal run-off. Coronary-coronary bypass on a beating heart without cardiopulmonary bypass can be performed safely in a patient with porcelain aorta.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
